# ── Key material (SEC-31, OPS-AUDIT-REMEDIATION-LOW-W1) ──────────────────────
# This was the bare literal `.env` and nothing else, so `.env.production`,
# `.env.local`, a stray `foo.pem` or an exported `*.key` were all committable into
# a PUBLIC repo with every gate green. SEC-02 — a live production Postgres password
# that sat in this repo for seven weeks — is what that costs. Patterns, not filenames.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
!*.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519
*.asc
*.gpg
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# ops/scripts host-map — keeps the private AOE IP out of this PUBLIC repo (OPS-HETZNER-DISK-RECLAIM-W1)
ops/scripts/.disk-forensics-hosts

# MCP registry publishing tokens (local-only, never commit)
.mcpregistry_github_token
.mcpregistry_registry_token

# Parallel-session worktrees (native `claude -w` / scripts/cc-session.sh)
/.claude/worktrees/

# Per-repo napkin runbook (`napkin` skill) — local-only working notes. Names the
# operator's own filesystem layout (/Users/…) and the prod container, and this repo
# is PUBLIC, so it stays out of git. Same class as ops/scripts/.disk-forensics-hosts
# above. NOTE: `.claude/` is only PARTIALLY ignored (worktrees + this file) — it
# still holds tracked files (settings.json, launch.json, commands/), so an
# unignored file here WOULD be swept in by `git add -A`, which CLAUDE.md bans for
# exactly this reason. (OPS-ENDPOINT-TRUTH-VAULT-QUARANTINE-W1)
/.claude/napkin.md
